1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,468
Sometimes you run the exactness test, and it fails.

2
00:00:04,468 --> 00:00:06,834
M sub y and N sub x do not match.

3
00:00:07,553 --> 00:00:12,933
But very often, the equation is just one multiplication away from being exact.

4
00:00:12,933 --> 00:00:16,379
We multiply the whole thing by an integrating factor,

5
00:00:16,379 --> 00:00:19,825
mu, and then it is an ordinary exact equation.

6
00:00:20,246 --> 00:00:22,760
The whole question is: how do you find mu?

7
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:24,752
Your lecture gives two quick tests.

8
00:00:28,352 --> 00:00:30,774
First, where do the tests come from?

9
00:00:30,774 --> 00:00:34,175
We want mu M, and mu N, to pass the exactness test.

10
00:00:34,595 --> 00:00:37,072
Suppose mu depends on x only.

11
00:00:37,072 --> 00:00:43,884
Then the left side is mu M sub y, and the right side is mu prime N, plus mu N sub x.

12
00:00:44,304 --> 00:00:50,733
Rearrange, and you get mu prime over mu, equals M sub y minus N sub x, over N.

13
00:00:51,152 --> 00:00:54,791
That only makes sense if the right side depends on x alone.

14
00:00:54,791 --> 00:00:59,588
If it does, integrate, and mu equals e to the integral of that quotient.

15
00:01:03,288 --> 00:01:07,843
So here are the two tests, exactly as they appear on the Lecture 4 slide.

16
00:01:08,263 --> 00:01:12,556
Test one. Compute M sub y minus N sub x, over N.

17
00:01:12,556 --> 00:01:18,866
If that is a function of x alone, then mu of x equals e to the integral of it, dx.

18
00:01:19,286 --> 00:01:23,654
Test two. Compute N sub x minus M sub y, over M.

19
00:01:23,654 --> 00:01:30,073
If that is a function of y alone, then mu of y equals e to the integral of it, dy.

20
00:01:30,493 --> 00:01:34,376
A way to remember it: divide by N, get a function of x.

21
00:01:34,376 --> 00:01:36,816
Divide by M, get a function of y.

22
00:01:36,816 --> 00:01:41,032
And the numerator always starts with the partial of the other letter.

23
00:01:44,731 --> 00:01:47,178
Now Example 3 from Lecture 4.

24
00:01:47,178 --> 00:01:53,770
x y dx, plus two x squared plus three y squared minus twenty, dy, equals zero.

25
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:56,994
M sub y is x. N sub x is four x.

26
00:01:56,994 --> 00:01:59,494
Not equal, so it is not exact.

27
00:01:59,914 --> 00:02:05,513
Try test one. M sub y minus N sub x, over N, is negative three x, over

28
00:02:05,513 --> 00:02:10,923
two x squared plus three y squared minus twenty. That still has y in it.

29
00:02:10,923 --> 00:02:12,307
Test one fails.

30
00:02:12,728 --> 00:02:18,259
Try test two. N sub x minus M sub y, over M, is three x over x y.

31
00:02:18,259 --> 00:02:23,345
The x cancels, and we get three over y. A function of y alone.

32
00:02:23,764 --> 00:02:27,085
So mu of y is e to the integral of three over y,

33
00:02:27,085 --> 00:02:31,684
which is e to the three natural log of y. That is y cubed.

34
00:02:35,584 --> 00:02:38,067
Multiply every term by y cubed.

35
00:02:38,067 --> 00:02:42,640
x y to the fourth dx, plus two x squared y cubed, plus three y

36
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:47,278
to the fifth, minus twenty y cubed, dy, equals zero.

37
00:02:47,699 --> 00:02:49,257
Always re-check.

38
00:02:49,257 --> 00:02:55,898
The new M sub y is four x y cubed, and the new N sub x is four x y cubed. Exact.

39
00:02:56,318 --> 00:03:02,045
Integrate M in x. f equals one half x squared y to the fourth, plus g of y.

40
00:03:02,465 --> 00:03:05,737
Differentiate in y, and set it equal to N.

41
00:03:05,737 --> 00:03:08,886
Two x squared y cubed cancels on both sides.

42
00:03:09,306 --> 00:03:13,441
So g prime is three y to the fifth minus twenty y cubed, and g

43
00:03:13,441 --> 00:03:17,576
is one half y to the sixth, minus five y to the fourth.

44
00:03:17,996 --> 00:03:22,570
The solution is the level curve: one half x squared y to the fourth, plus

45
00:03:22,570 --> 00:03:26,919
one half y to the sixth, minus five y to the fourth, equals c.

46
00:03:31,019 --> 00:03:31,902
Three traps.

47
00:03:32,322 --> 00:03:34,632
One. Mixing up the quotients.

48
00:03:34,632 --> 00:03:39,038
If you divide by N, you need a function of x, and you integrate dx.

49
00:03:39,038 --> 00:03:43,443
If you divide by M, you need a function of y, and you integrate dy.

50
00:03:43,863 --> 00:03:47,303
Two. Multiplying only part of the equation.

51
00:03:47,303 --> 00:03:50,442
mu multiplies M and N, every single term.

52
00:03:50,862 --> 00:03:53,006
Three. Skipping the re-check.

53
00:03:53,006 --> 00:03:57,295
Ten seconds to confirm the new equation is exact, saves the whole question.

54
00:04:01,095 --> 00:04:02,359
Here is the recipe.

55
00:04:02,779 --> 00:04:04,255
One. Show the test fails.

56
00:04:04,675 --> 00:04:08,248
Two. Try the quotient over N for a function of x.

57
00:04:08,248 --> 00:04:11,594
If not, the quotient over M for a function of y.

58
00:04:12,014 --> 00:04:15,622
Three. Build mu, multiply every term, and re-check.

59
00:04:16,042 --> 00:04:21,287
Four. Solve it as an exact equation, and finish with f of x y, equals c.

60
00:04:21,707 --> 00:04:24,864
Next up, Bernoulli equations, where one clever

61
00:04:24,864 --> 00:04:28,664
substitution turns a nonlinear equation into a linear one.
